                <p class="dateAesteticTitle2">PRESS RELEASE <br>Aug 17, 2026</p>
                <h3>Contact Hours: The cost of unspoken pain</h3>
                <p class="dateAesteticTitle2">By Carla Aguilar López</p>

            </div>
            <div class="col s12 m3"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
            <div class="col s12 m8">
                <img class="responsive-img" src="/assets/images/contactInterview.webp" style="border-radius: 25px ;">
                <p class="dateAesteticTitle2 center">An interview with Harry Richards and Rufus Love by Carla Aguilar
                    López </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
            <div class="col s12 m8 paragraphStyleNews">
                <p>I had the pleasure of chatting today with director Harry Richards and writer/actor Rufus Love about
                    their film Contact hours, which they both produced through their production company, Headrush Films.
                    This is not their first time working together, but it is their first time on the platform Vilpa Max.
                    Contact Hours premiered on the platform this week, and we couldn’t be more excited about it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    From the very beginning, the interview felt warm, not only because Harry, Rufus, and I had briefly
                    met in the past at the Cannes Film Festival, but also because they are two of the loveliest people.
                    They met Alejandro at Cannes last year and reconnected this past May at the festival. As Vilpa Max
                    was already a reality, they discussed the possibility of having the film on the platform and there
                    they are. This is how connections in this industry work.
                </p>
                <img class="responsive-img" src="/assets/images/rufusnharry.webp" style="border-radius: 25px ;">
                <p>But moving on to the movie, Contact Hours is a deeply moving film that treats a subject as painfully
                    human as suicide in a very respectful way. The film follows a university caretaker who discovers
                    that a student in his accommodation block has taken his own life.
                </p>
                <p>The idea for this film came about when Rufus had a conversation at a bar with a man who was there to
                    do some maintenance work:
                </p>
                <p style="font-style: italic;">“I asked if he had been doing this job for a while, and he answered that
                    he previously worked as a
                    caretaker in a University, but he had to leave his position because he'd had the unfortunate
                    experience of finding more than one student who had taken their own life."</p>
                <img class="responsive-img" src="/assets/images/stillContact.webp" style="border-radius: 25px ;">

                <p>In the film, everything unfolds from the perspective of the caretaker. One of the students mentions
                    that they haven’t seen their colleague for a couple of days, and the caretaker goes to his room to
                    check on him. At the same time, there’s the relationship between him and his son, and this whole
                    situation puts the problems in that relationship into perspective.
                </p>
                <p>I asked Harry what made him want to jump into this story.</p>
                <p style="font-style: italic;">“I really felt passionately about the subject because very sadly we lost
                    some people in our
                    university cohort to suicide, and I remember actually before Rufus sent me the script, I had written
                    down a note on my phone of the names of the people that had died by suicide. I couldn't believe that
                    this was happening and that no one was really talking about it. So then when Rufus sent me this very
                    beautiful screenplay, I felt like it was an amazing opportunity to use a film to shed light on this
                    issue and hopefully, you know, with it bring about more conversations around suicide in university
                    communities in the UK”.
                </p>
                <p>Harry continues, <span style="font-style: italic;"> “I also felt that stylistically it felt in a
                        similar wheelhouse to the film I had
                        made previously, Santi, while also giving me an opportunity as a director to grow and to try new
                        things and to lean into a style that maybe I was interested in exploring. I felt that this was a
                        film that because of its social message, there was potential for us to raise quite a lot of
                        money to
                        make it, which we were able to do and we really created a beautiful community around the film
                        with
                        the charity partners, the universities, the people involved. It was a special project”.</span>
                </p>
                <p> <span style="font-style: italic;">“The film begins with the disappearance of a student but evolves
                        into a story about a father and son.
                        What drew you to that parallel?”</span>, I asked next.
                </p>
                <p>Rufus replied: <span style="font-style: italic;">“You have this kind of pastoral relationship where
                        he is a student and somebody's son
                        and the caretaker is a father but he's also a kind of father to all of the students who live in
                        his
                        accommodation block. He's got that kind of caring paternal role. So early in the film when the
                        student in 319 dies, our caretaker loses a son in a certain respect, like one of his children.
                        That
                        gives him the opportunity to save another of his children, which happens to be his biological
                        son,
                        who we learn is kind of going through some difficult stuff at the beginning of the film”.</span>
                </p>
                <p>About the filming process of such an emotionally charged film, Harry explained that the 25-minute
                    film was shot in just three days, making it essential to create an environment where the actors
                    could safely access the emotional vulnerability required by the story. While the subject matter was
                    dark and challenging, the production prioritized well-being and support, ensuring the cast felt
                    comfortable exploring difficult emotions. At the same time, the team worked hard to maintain a
                    positive and hopeful atmosphere on set, encouraging moments of laughter and connection so that the
                    filmmaking process never became overwhelmed by the heaviness of its themes.
                </p>
                <p>They recalled a particular scene in which the parents of the student come to the accommodation block
                    looking for their son’ belongings. On that same day, both Rufus’ and Harry’s parents were visiting
                    the set, and it was an incredibly emotional experience for them.
                </p>
                <p>For this scene, they told us, <span style="font-style: italic;">“We'd spoken to mothers and fathers
                        who had lost their child to suicide
                        and all of the very unique and difficult ways that grief manifests”</span>, and recalled that
                    watching the
                    scene was very hard because the set reminded them so much of their own student accommodation. It
                    felt incredibly real to them and, at the same time, made them feel grateful for their lives.
                </p>
                <p>I also asked about the somewhat paranormal element in the film involving a blue light, as well as the
                    representation of the corridor. They explained that the recurring blue light was intended as a
                    surreal visual device to represent the pain, confusion, and disorientation that follow a suicide
                    loss. Rather than offering a literal explanation, they wanted viewers to experience the Caretaker’s
                    fractured emotional state, where guilt, grief, and questions about what could have been done
                    differently blur the boundaries between past and present. Inspired in part by The Father starring
                    Anthony Hopkins, the film places the audience inside the protagonist’s subjective experience,
                    creating a dreamlike atmosphere in which time, space, and memory become unstable as he struggles to
                    process trauma.</p>
                <img class="responsive-img" src="/assets/images/contactStillOne.webp" style="border-radius: 25px ;">
                <p>As for that long corridor, which appears in many scenes and is always beautifully shot, with the
                    camera almost floating through it, I personally see it as two things: on one hand, it can represent
                    the choices we have in life, all those doors leading to different paths. On the other hand, those
                    doors remain closed. They never open during the film except for one moment, perhaps symbolizing that
                    there is no escape, that the doors won’t open for you no matter how hard you try. This could very
                    well be the point of view of the student.</p>
                <p><span style="font-style: italic;">“Definitely”,</span> Harry says and continues: <span
                        style="font-style: italic;">“I think it was firstly something that was in the script from
                        the start, which was this idea of doors being a symbol of connection and the open door
                        symbolizing
                        more of a connection than a closed door. And I think it was a very visceral memory of ours from
                        university which was this idea that I lived in this block where you had these very strong fire
                        doors
                        and from a practical point of view they're very effective but from an emotional point of view
                        they're actually pretty horrible because as soon as that that door shuts it blocks out all of
                        the
                        sound and suddenly you feel very isolated”.</span>
                </p>
                <p>At one moment during the interview, Rufus explained something that I found profoundly beautiful: the
                    student in Room 319 was intentionally left largely unknown, serving as a symbolic figure rather than
                    an individual character. Inspired by the concept of the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, the student
                    represents the many young people who have lost their lives to suicide, giving a face to those whose
                    stories often remain untold and whose loss is felt far beyond a single family or community.
                </p>
                <p>The words that Rufus and Harry use to describe the film are hopeful, poetic, lingering, and
                    affecting, and I couldn’t agree more.
                </p>
                <p>Take a look at this short film. It will leave you reflecting on life for hours.</p>
